  "worked_solution": "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & 10 \\\\  & \u2193 & -5 & -10 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}=x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+2x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}]+2[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
